WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:00.510
SETH VARGO: All right.

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.230
Welcome back.

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:03.000
Today I have Sandeep with me.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.890
Sandeep is a Developer Advocate.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.010
What does a Developer
Advocate do?

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:09.439
SANDEEP PARIKH: Yes, so
Developer Advocates at Google,

00:00:09.439 --> 00:00:10.980
and I think at a
lot of other places,

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.770
are engineers who get to
engage with the community.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:18.180
So they get to participate
by speaking at conferences,

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.159
writing cool documentation, code
samples, basically giving back.

00:00:22.159 --> 00:00:23.700
And also learning
from the community,

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.800
like developers and
operators, and using

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.070
that to improve products.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:27.490
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:28.830
So they're not like
marketing people?

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:29.621
SANDEEP PARIKH: No.

00:00:29.621 --> 00:00:31.980
They're very much intended
to be like engineers

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.230
who have come from that
ecosystem or that environment.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:37.020
So former developers, operators.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.360
Specifically within
Kubernetes, like definitely

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.807
cluster operators,
sysadmins, DevOps,

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:43.890
those kinds of folks with
that kind of background.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:44.190
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:45.960
So what exactly does
a Developer Advocate

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.610
do in the Kubernetes ecosystem?

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:48.740
What kind of things
are you involved in?

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:49.430
SANDEEP PARIKH: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:51.990
So the name is sort
of funny, because I

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.030
think Developer Advocate
implies developer only,

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.460
when really, within the
Kubernetes ecosystem,

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.720
it's developer, DevOps, pure
ops, and kind of further

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:01.950
and further down the stack.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.840
So within the
Kubernetes ecosystem,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.540
I think people are getting
to participate by one,

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.210
helping build better
code samples around just

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.524
using Kubernetes for operators.

00:01:10.524 --> 00:01:11.940
But also going
further and further

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.250
up the stack with new
projects and tools

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:15.840
that are very developer-centric.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.140
Which Kubernetes by itself is
not a developer-facing tool

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:19.987
in a lot of ways.

00:01:19.987 --> 00:01:20.820
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:24.350
So walk me through an
average day for you.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.190
SANDEEP PARIKH:
Yeah, so a lot of it

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:30.330
really is, well, one, nonstop
email checking and group chat

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:31.770
participation.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:35.250
And then filing bug
reports on products

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.740
that are part of Google
Cloud, writing code samples,

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.987
writing documentation,
updating code samples

00:01:39.987 --> 00:01:42.570
and documentation, because those
get out of date very quickly.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:45.437
And answering PRs on GitHub
and helping merge in new code.

00:01:45.437 --> 00:01:47.520
SETH VARGO: So I'm sure
everyone is dying to know,

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.622
how many Slack channels
are you a part of?

00:01:49.622 --> 00:01:52.080
SANDEEP PARIKH: That's a number
I will never share publicly

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.450
because it would frighten
and confuse most people.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:56.970
SETH VARGO: So you said you
work a lot with the ecosystem.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.422
What are some really exciting
ecosystem tools and kind

00:02:00.422 --> 00:02:02.130
of patterns that are
emerging that you're

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.150
excited about, personally?

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:03.550
SANDEEP PARIKH: Yeah, no.

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.133
I mean, I think
what's really cool is,

00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:08.310
I was at the first
KubeCon back in 2015,

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.250
when it was 300 people in
one room in the Palace Hotel.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.770
And it's grown incredibly
since that time,

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.170
in the last three and 1/2 years.

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:17.670
And what's really cool is, it's
gone further and further up.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.530
So we're getting
closer and closer

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.127
to like end
application developers.

00:02:21.127 --> 00:02:22.710
So I'm really excited
about the things

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.205
that make application
developers' lives easier.

00:02:25.205 --> 00:02:27.330
So some of the really cool
tools-- and a little bit

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.079
self-serving, because
they're Google tools

00:02:29.079 --> 00:02:31.410
that we're working on-- but
I think Scaffold and Jib

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.910
are really cool tools.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.740
And then something
like Knative, which

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.930
isn't exactly
developer-facing, but it's

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.360
a project which is going to
make it easier for application

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.520
developers to get a lot
of these full benefits

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.360
without having to
write YAML and remember

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.910
v1beta1 or v1alpha1 APIs.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:49.140
SETH VARGO: Exactly.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.800
So let's say I'm new to the
Kubernetes community, right?

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:53.770
You said 300.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:56.010
We're at like, what, 8,000
people at this conference?

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.870
The community is
growing dramatically.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.000
If I'm new to this
thing, where do I start?

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.640
Where are some areas that I
can make an impact, maybe even

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.220
if I don't have a background in
software or computer science?

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.780
SANDEEP PARIKH: Yeah,
you know, everybody

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.113
has a unique experience to
bring, which is what I found.

00:03:09.113 --> 00:03:12.480
And just hearing talks from
across the community today,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.250
and in the last couple
of days at KubeCon,

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.166
everybody's got something
compelling to share.

00:03:16.166 --> 00:03:19.600
I think there's tons of getting
started resources out there.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.770
But there's never
enough applied use case.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:25.590
So taking the experience
that you've had,

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.756
even if it was without
Kubernetes, and saying, well,

00:03:27.756 --> 00:03:30.240
how would I map that to
a Kubernetes environment

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.230
or an environment with Istio or
with any of the other thousands

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.500
of tools that are out there?

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:36.570
You know, rethinking
through that

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:38.778
and then sharing that story
is still pretty valuable,

00:03:38.778 --> 00:03:41.100
because I think it helps
everybody try to map something

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.310
new in their brain.

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:44.740
I just went to a session that
was about PCI compliance,

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:45.240
right?

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.040
So processing credit card data--

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:48.720
something we've
been doing for years

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:52.200
in traditional environments,
but not as much in Kubernetes.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.480
And that's really valuable
for the community to learn.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:55.313
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:03:55.313 --> 00:03:56.170
So share stories.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:57.420
SANDEEP PARIKH: Yeah, exactly.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.003
Share stories.

00:03:58.003 --> 00:04:00.210
And it doesn't have to be code.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.460
It doesn't have to be
an innovative thing.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.552
It just has to be
something that's, I think,

00:04:04.552 --> 00:04:06.510
positive in helping move
the community forward.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:07.680
And that could just
be a simple thing

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.210
that you've learned
that you want to share.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:09.810
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:10.730
Well, thank you, Sandeep.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:11.190
SANDEEP PARIKH: All right.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:11.970
Thank you.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

